    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The ratio between the perimeters of two similar figures is equal to the ratio of corresponding side lengths. We have a ratio of  $\\displaystyle \\frac{ \\text{new perimeter}}{6 + 7 + 9}= \\frac{110}{22} =5$\nThus we have $5 = \\frac{ \\text{longest side of new figure}}{4}$\nTherefore the longest side of the new figure is $5*4 = \\boxed{20}$ centimeters. To determine the length of the longest side of the similar triangle, we first need to calculate the perimeter of the original triangle. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of its three sides. For the original triangle with sides 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm, the perimeter is:\n\n\\[\n6 + 7 + 9 = 22 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the perimeter of the similar triangle is 110 cm. To find the ratio of the perimeters of the two triangles, we divide the perimeter of the similar triangle by the perimeter of the original triangle:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{110 \\text{ cm}}{22 \\text{ cm}} = 5\n\\]\n\nThis means that the ratio of the sides of the two triangles is also 5. The longest side of the original triangle is 9 cm. Corresponding to this longest side in the original triangle is the longest side in the similar triangle, which is 5 times the length of the longest side of the original triangle. Therefore, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is:\n\n\\[\n9 \\text{ cm} \\times 5 = 45 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nSince the longest side of any triangle is given by the longest side of the original triangle, which is 9 cm, the length of the longest side of the new triangle is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{45}\n\\]",
